{"subjectId":"sql","id":"update-delete","title":"UPDATE & DELETE","group":"Defining & Changing Data","order":9,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>UPDATE</code> changes column values in existing rows; <code>DELETE</code> removes rows. Both touch <strong>every row the <code>WHERE</code> matches</strong> — which is all of them if you forget it.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-pencil\"></i>UPDATE</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- 10% raise for Sales; several columns can change at once\nUPDATE employees\nSET salary = salary * 1.10,\n    email  = COALESCE(email, lower(name) || '@corp.in')\nWHERE dept_id = 2;\n\nSELECT emp_id, name, email, salary FROM employees WHERE dept_id = 2 ORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>emp_id | name  | email         | salary\n-------+-------+---------------+-------------------\n5      | Priya | priya@corp.in | 121000.00000000001\n6      | Arjun | arjun@corp.in | 77000\n7      | Neha  | neha@corp.in  | 79200</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Every expression on the right of <code>SET</code> sees the row's <strong>old</strong> values, so <code>SET a = b, b = a</code> swaps two columns in standard SQL (MySQL is the odd one out — it applies assignments left to right).</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Priya's <code>121000.00000000001</code> is floating-point error from <code>* 1.10</code> (SQLite stored a <code>REAL</code>). In a real schema <code>salary</code> would be <code>DECIMAL(10,2)</code>; in SQLite wrap it: <code>CAST(round(salary * 1.10) AS INTEGER)</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table-import\"></i>UPDATE using another table</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- raise everyone located in Hyderabad by 5000: subquery form (works everywhere)\nUPDATE employees\nSET salary = salary + 5000\nWHERE dept_id IN (SELECT dept_id FROM departments WHERE location = 'Hyderabad');\n\nSELECT name, salary FROM employees WHERE dept_id = 3 ORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | salary\n-------+-------\nVikram | 85000\nSara   | 90000</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- UPDATE … FROM (PostgreSQL, SQLite 3.33+): join the table being updated\nCREATE TABLE raises (emp_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, pct INTEGER);\nINSERT INTO raises VALUES (3, 10), (4, 20);\n\nUPDATE employees\nSET salary = salary + salary * r.pct / 100\nFROM raises AS r\nWHERE r.emp_id = employees.emp_id;\n\nSELECT name, salary FROM employees WHERE emp_id IN (3, 4) ORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | salary\n------+-------\nMeera | 104500\nKaran | 114000</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>MySQL / SQL Server</figcaption><pre><code>-- MySQL: multi-table UPDATE with JOIN\nUPDATE employees e\nJOIN raises r ON r.emp_id = e.emp_id\nSET e.salary = e.salary + e.salary * r.pct / 100;\n\n-- SQL Server: UPDATE … FROM with an alias\nUPDATE e SET e.salary = e.salary + e.salary * r.pct / 100\nFROM employees e JOIN raises r ON r.emp_id = e.emp_id;</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trash\"></i>DELETE</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- remove order lines of cancelled orders, then the orders themselves\nDELETE FROM order_items\nWHERE order_id IN (SELECT order_id FROM orders WHERE status = 'cancelled');\n\nDELETE FROM orders WHERE status = 'cancelled'\nRETURNING order_id, customer_id;\n\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS orders_left FROM orders;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>order_id | customer_id\n---------+------------\n105      | 4\n\norders_left\n-----------\n7</code></pre></figure>\n<p>The child rows (<code>order_items</code>) had to go first — otherwise the foreign key would block deleting the order (unless it was declared <code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code>).</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield\"></i>Safe habits</h3>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Preview</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Run <code>SELECT … WHERE &lt;same condition&gt;</code> first and check the row count.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Wrap in a transaction</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>BEGIN; UPDATE …;</code> → check the result → <code>COMMIT</code> or <code>ROLLBACK</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Limit the blast radius</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Key the <code>WHERE</code> on the primary key when you mean one row.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Prefer soft deletes for business data</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>UPDATE … SET deleted_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</code> keeps history and is reversible.</div></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>BEGIN;\nUPDATE employees SET salary = 1;          -- oops: no WHERE, all 10 rows changed\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS paid_one_rupee FROM employees WHERE salary = 1;\nROLLBACK;                                 -- undo everything since BEGIN\nSELECT name, salary FROM employees WHERE emp_id = 1;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>paid_one_rupee\n--------------\n10\n\nname | salary\n-----+-------\nAsha | 150000</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you delete rows from one table based on another table?</summary><div><p>Use a subquery — <code>DELETE FROM a WHERE id IN (SELECT a_id FROM b WHERE …)</code> or <code>WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM b WHERE b.a_id = a.id …)</code>. MySQL and SQL Server also allow a join form: <code>DELETE a FROM a JOIN b ON … WHERE …</code>; PostgreSQL has <code>DELETE FROM a USING b WHERE …</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What happens if you run UPDATE without WHERE?</summary><div><p>Every row in the table is updated. If you are inside an explicit transaction you can <code>ROLLBACK</code>; in autocommit mode the change is permanent and you need a backup or point-in-time recovery. MySQL's <code>--safe-updates</code> mode refuses <code>UPDATE</code>/<code>DELETE</code> without a key-based <code>WHERE</code> or <code>LIMIT</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}